00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.680
Now here is something that most retail investors get wrong and it cost me money before I figured it out.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:30.180
Imagine you bought XEQT last week when the TSX posted modest gains. Your order filled at the market price

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.540
but here is what actually happened between your click and that fill.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:40.520
The ETFs underlying mix of equities and bonds rebalanced internally as global equity weights

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:48.380
shifted 0.4% that day. Modern portfolio theory shows that combining uncorrelated assets like Canadian

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:55.020
equities US large caps and bonds moves you closer to the efficient frontier where expected returns

00:08:55.020 --> 00:09:02.440
stays the same while volatility drops 15-20%. Strategic allocation sets long-term targets by

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:08.540
risk tolerance while tactical shifts of 5% or less let you respond to events like today's energy

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:15.640
rotation without over trading. Bond still reduced drawdowns post-2022 because their negative correlation

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:23.500
with stocks reappears once rate volatility subsides. Age-based rules like 60% equities at age 40

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:30.620
are oversimplified. Instead run a quick risk quiz on your broker's platform and set a 5% threshold

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:36.760
for rebalancing. The biggest mistake with asset allocation is ignoring correlation drift after a

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:44.360
strong equity year. Instead always review your XBAL or VGRO holdings quarterly against target

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:51.400
weights and rebalance only when any sleeve drifts beyond 5%. Before we wrap, open your brokerage

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:58.700
screener tomorrow and filter TSX energy names for upgrades in the past week and volume above the 20-day

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:04.980
average. That's your modern investing techniques for today. Every episode makes you a sharper investor.

